WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.938
Welcome to Episode 3 of the Start series
from the Federal Modernization Workbook.

2
00:00:04.938 --> 00:00:06.929
Episode 2 ended at gate G zero,

3
00:00:06.929 --> 00:00:11.326
with the estate inventoried and
the plans about to be generated.

4
00:00:11.326 --> 00:00:13.863
Now the work reaches the live environment.

5
00:00:13.863 --> 00:00:18.795
Over roughly four weeks, Phase one puts
the identity building blocks in place:

6
00:00:18.795 --> 00:00:23.496
groups and scoped administration, a
sign-in baseline, emergency accounts,

7
00:00:23.496 --> 00:00:28.813
the first HR feed, address management, and
the first servers under cloud management.

8
00:00:28.813 --> 00:00:31.450
Then gate G one checks
all of it from data.

9
00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:33.440
Let's see how the pieces fit.

10
00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:39.552
Phase one has one goal: put the identity
basics in place before anything else moves.

11
00:00:39.552 --> 00:00:41.538
It opens with the plans approved,

12
00:00:41.538 --> 00:00:45.364
and then five workstreams move
together, not one after another.

13
00:00:45.364 --> 00:00:49.690
Identity builds groups, scoped
administration and the HR feed.

14
00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:52.027
Policy sets the sign-in baseline.

15
00:00:52.027 --> 00:00:55.952
Services brings the core infrastructure
under address management.

16
00:00:55.952 --> 00:00:58.215
Compute connects the first servers.

17
00:00:58.215 --> 00:01:02.615
And access opens strong sign-in for
the most privileged administrators.

18
00:01:02.615 --> 00:01:04.827
Running them in parallel is the point.

19
00:01:04.827 --> 00:01:07.100
First, groups and scope.

20
00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:12.563
In the old directory, where an account sat
in the tree decided who could manage it.

21
00:01:12.563 --> 00:01:16.938
The cloud directory is flat, so
scope has to be declared on purpose.

22
00:01:16.938 --> 00:01:21.415
Phase one does that with dynamic
groups and administrative units (AUs),

23
00:01:21.415 --> 00:01:25.662
whose membership comes from attributes
like department, fed by HR.

24
00:01:25.662 --> 00:01:26.788
Two things to know.

25
00:01:26.788 --> 00:01:30.213
AUs limit who can change an
account, not who can see it.

26
00:01:30.213 --> 00:01:32.577
And membership updates take hours,

27
00:01:32.577 --> 00:01:36.163
sometimes more than a day, so
plan in hours, not minutes.

28
00:01:36.163 --> 00:01:39.800
Next, the baseline every
sign-in has to meet.

29
00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:44.875
The sign-in baseline is short:
multifactor authentication for everyone,

30
00:01:44.875 --> 00:01:46.750
legacy sign-in methods blocked,

31
00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:51.075
and emergency accounts excluded from
anything that could lock them out.

32
00:01:51.075 --> 00:01:56.013
Blocking legacy authentication is also
a mandatory federal cloud baseline.

33
00:01:56.013 --> 00:01:58.387
What matters most is how it's switched on.

34
00:01:58.387 --> 00:02:02.459
Every policy runs in report-only
mode first, for at least a week,

35
00:02:02.459 --> 00:02:05.812
so you can see who would be
blocked before anyone is.

36
00:02:05.812 --> 00:02:07.837
Then it's enforced ring by ring,

37
00:02:07.837 --> 00:02:11.400
and rolled back by turning it
off, never by deleting it.

38
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.210
That makes the emergency
accounts critical.

39
00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:20.313
Emergency access accounts are how you
get back in when everything else fails:

40
00:02:20.313 --> 00:02:23.073
sign-in, multifactor, or a policy mistake.

41
00:02:23.073 --> 00:02:25.882
They're the only standing
administrator rights the

42
00:02:25.882 --> 00:02:28.822
program accepts by design,
so they're governed tightly.

43
00:02:28.822 --> 00:02:33.086
At least two cloud-only accounts,
with phishing-resistant sign-in,

44
00:02:33.086 --> 00:02:38.060
excluded from blocking policies through
one group, and an alert on every use.

45
00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:40.498
They're tested at least every ninety days.

46
00:02:40.498 --> 00:02:44.752
And they must work before any
administrator role becomes just-in-time,

47
00:02:44.752 --> 00:02:47.760
or the agency can lock itself
out of its own tenant.

48
00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:50.200
Next, the first HR feed.

49
00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:56.163
This is where HR starts to drive identity,
for a first, limited group of people.

50
00:02:56.163 --> 00:02:57.413
The order matters.

51
00:02:57.413 --> 00:03:01.950
The feed is named as one of the
agency's HR integration points.

52
00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:04.407
The attribute authority map is signed,

53
00:03:04.407 --> 00:03:08.323
and HR data is checked against
reality and fixed at the source,

54
00:03:08.323 --> 00:03:13.237
because a drifted feed produces drifted
access faster than any ticket queue.

55
00:03:13.237 --> 00:03:17.925
Joiner, mover and leaver are proven
end to end in a test tenant.

56
00:03:17.925 --> 00:03:20.346
Then the pilot group runs in shadow mode,

57
00:03:20.346 --> 00:03:24.440
where the feed shows what it would
do, before it's allowed to act.

58
00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:27.953
Two infrastructure pieces
join the foundation.

59
00:03:27.953 --> 00:03:29.777
First, address management.

60
00:03:29.777 --> 00:03:32.767
Many agencies track IP
ranges in a spreadsheet,

61
00:03:32.767 --> 00:03:36.216
and that's where conflicts
and ghost records come from.

62
00:03:36.216 --> 00:03:41.431
Phase one puts the platform host and
the most critical infrastructure under

63
00:03:41.431 --> 00:03:46.240
one governed address authority, so
DNS and DHCP can later derive from it.

64
00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:47.802
Second, servers.

65
00:03:47.802 --> 00:03:50.180
Intune doesn't manage Windows Server,

66
00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:54.628
so the platform host and a few pilot
servers connect through Azure Arc.

67
00:03:54.628 --> 00:03:57.599
Each gets a cloud identity,
a security baseline,

68
00:03:57.599 --> 00:04:02.160
patching and protection, without
changing its domain membership.

69
00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:07.848
Gate G one has a short list, and each
line is a query, not an opinion.

70
00:04:07.848 --> 00:04:12.159
Groups and administrative units
match their registry, with no drift.

71
00:04:12.159 --> 00:04:17.160
The sign-in logs show no one outside
multifactor except the emergency accounts.

72
00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:20.573
The emergency accounts
have recent, logged tests.

73
00:04:20.573 --> 00:04:23.872
The address inventory covers
the critical infrastructure.

74
00:04:23.872 --> 00:04:28.147
The provisioning logs show the HR feed
running for the foundation group.

75
00:04:28.147 --> 00:04:30.723
And high findings are
fixed and re-scanned.

76
00:04:30.723 --> 00:04:34.236
The steward decides, but only
after the data says green.

77
00:04:34.236 --> 00:04:35.850
So what can go wrong?

78
00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:39.075
Most foundation failures are predictable.

79
00:04:39.075 --> 00:04:43.704
A broad multifactor policy blocks
an unusual but critical sign-in,

80
00:04:43.704 --> 00:04:46.987
which is why everything
runs report-only first.

81
00:04:46.987 --> 00:04:50.026
An administrator role goes
just-in-time before the

82
00:04:50.026 --> 00:04:53.426
emergency accounts work, and
the tenant locks itself out.

83
00:04:53.426 --> 00:04:58.375
A policy aimed at a group silently misses
everyone whose attribute is empty.

84
00:04:58.375 --> 00:05:01.500
The HR feed automates
on data nobody checked.

85
00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:05.282
And if the HR connector breaks
mid-rollout, the program stalls,

86
00:05:05.282 --> 00:05:08.025
which is why the gate
requires the feed first.

87
00:05:08.025 --> 00:05:11.910
Rollback triggers halt the
work before a problem spreads.

88
00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:16.960
When gate G one passes, leadership
can see a foundation that holds.

89
00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:19.422
Everyone signs in with multifactor.

90
00:05:19.422 --> 00:05:24.209
Administration is scoped by attributes
instead of a tree nobody wrote down.

91
00:05:24.209 --> 00:05:27.135
Emergency access is governed and tested.

92
00:05:27.135 --> 00:05:30.324
HR is driving identity for
a first group of people,

93
00:05:30.324 --> 00:05:35.223
and the core servers and addresses are
under management, all with zero drift.

94
00:05:35.223 --> 00:05:37.761
That's what the pilot
division builds on next.

95
00:05:37.761 --> 00:05:41.948
The last episode steps back from
the mechanics to the meeting itself.

96
00:05:41.948 --> 00:05:47.890
Episode 4, Making the Case at Your Agency,
turns all of this into a one-page ask.
